  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Convection earlier this morning continues to move \neast and away from the area early this afternoon.  A cool front also \ncontinues to push southeast, already well into NW Iowa.  Behind this \nboundary, periods of very low stratus with drizzle have been \nreported. As stronger dry air advection moves southeast, we should \ncontinue to see stratus erode. Temperatures with all the cloud \ncover have been stuck in the upper 60s to lower 70s, with a \nsmall areas ahead of the front in the lower 80s. As long as \nclouds clear by mid-afternoon, there should be enough time for \nhighs to still reach the upper 70s to lower 80s.\n\nTONIGHT: High level wildfire smoke will continue to leave a milky \nsky overhead.  However we've seen little impact on overnight lows \nwith this smoke, so with the continued dry air advection southward \nthis afternoon and evening, we are likely to see low temperatures in \nthe lower 50s by Saturday morning. One area to watch after midnight \nwill be in central Nebraska into central South Dakota, as guidance \ncontinues to hint as a subtle wave entering the Dakotas.  This wave \nshould induce a stronger LLJ which in return may produce enough \nfocused convergence for a few stronger thunderstorms. The general \ntrack of any strong activity should be south southeast along the \nelevated instability axis into east central Nebraska. Nevertheless, \nif a storm could develop near Gregory County, 1.5\" hail would be the \nmain risk. \n\nSATURDAY:  Forecast confidence remains lower for Saturday regarding \nprecipitation chances.  Guidance is quite split on where the axis of \nstronger low-lvl jet convergence will drift through the day.   Most \nof this uncertainty is due to poor model resolution of any weak \nvorticity passing through the 700:500 layer. Some models keep the \nbest lift bottled up south of the Missouri River with an MCS moving \ntowards western Iowa.  Others have a wave further north, pushing the \nLLJ towards I-29 by mid-day and leaving a few lingering showers and \nclouds overhead into the afternoon.  What fairly certain is that \nMUCAPE will struggle to lift northeast, keeping any very isolated \nsevere weather risk focused along or south of the MO river into mid-\nday.  By the late afternoon and evening, the instability pool does \ndrift northward, however soundings also show a fairly stout capping \nlayer overhead that should inhibit convection given weak and \ntransient lift.  One note: If we do end up with at least a thick mid-\nlvl cloud field over the heart of the CWA by mid-day, then high \ntemperatures could be significantly impacted and convection risks \nlower even further. Rain chances may continue Saturday night, though \nfocus moreso over Iowa and western Minnesota as the LLJ redevelops \nand interactions with a developing elevated frontal boundary. \nConfidence is very low with these precipitation risks. \n\nSUNDAY: Any nocturnal convection Saturday night could play a role in \nredevelopment on Sunday. Again though, the myriad of potential small \nshortwaves moving through the NW flow is problematic with model \nguidance which shows a wide range of solutions.  Some guidance shows \na mostly dry day and night with subsidence and high pressure moving \nthrough.  Others maintain a surface and elevated frontal boundary in \nthe area, with increasing lift late in the day and some isolated \nstrong to severe storms.   Given the spread in models, low \nprobabilities spread through the evening and early overnight models \ncontinues to be the best way to suggest the potential at this stage. \n\nMONDAY-THURSDAY: There will really be no change in the forecast into \nthe middle of the week as the region remains under slight \nnorthwesterly flow aloft.  This flow will keep a persistent risk for \ndaily convection, though with extremely low confidence and \npredictability until a day or so before. There continues to be \nsome focus for lift around the Monday night/Tuesday and again \nWednesday night and Thursday timeframe where an MCS could form. \nTemperatures may fall back slightly into the lower to middle 80s\nnear the seasonal normals, with potential for a larger cooldown\nby next weekend.",
